essage: string;\n    status: 'OPEN' | 'RESOLVED';\n}\n```\n\n## Data Model\n\n| Entity | Attributes | Relationships |\n|:---|:---|:---|\n| Message | id, timestamp, from, to, phase, type, message, ref_doc, status | One-to-Many with State Transitions |\n| Workspace State | currentPhase, activeAgent, pendingTasks | Updated by Orchestrator based on Messages |\n\n## Infrastructure & Deployment\n- **Environment:** Runs purely as a VS Code extension inside the user's IDE.\n- **Deployment:** Packaged as a `.vsix` file using VS Code Extension tooling (`vsce`).\n- **Cloud Costs:** €0.00 (Local execution, relying on client-provided LLM endpoints).\n\n## Phased Implementation Roadmap\n\n> **CRITICAL WORKFLOW RULE:** Tasks are executed strictly one-by-one. We will not proceed to the next task until the current task is completed, validated, and approved. No subsequent tasks will be drafted until Task 1 is validated.\n\n### Phase 1: Custom State Machine\n\n#### Task 1: Core Custom State Machine Bootstrapping\n- **Estimated Hours:** 16 hours\n- **Dependencies:** None\n- **Required Agents:** \n  - `agency-lead-developer`: Orchestrates the implementation, ensures vertical task execution.\n  - `agency-developer`: Implements the FSM and writes the code/tests.\n  - `agency-qa`: Audits the code for security and architecture alignment.\n- **Explicit Skills Needed:**\n  - TypeScript (strict typing, enum creation).\n  - Finite State Machine (FSM) theory and implementation.\n  - Test-Driven Development (TDD).\n  - Deep Module Design (John Ousterhout principles).\n- **Technical Context & SOTA Reference:** SOTA Research - Global Orchestration & Workflow (Explicit FSM over Prompt Chaining) & Professional Coding Standards.\n\n**Detailed Functional Description:**\nImplement the foundational lightweight, custom Finite State Machine (FSM) in the orchestration engine. This code must be written in TypeScript natively within the VS Code extension. The engine must enforce deterministic, vertical task execution by rigorously governing the Devio agency's core lifecycle phases: `Brief`, `Proposal`, `Architecture`, `Development`, `Review`, `Delivery`, and `Done`. The FSM must explicitly reject any attempt by an agent to bypass sequential requirements.\n\n**Granular Implementation Details:**\n1. **Directory Structure & Ubiquitous Language:** Create an isolated domain model representing the state machine. Use strictly defined TypeScript enums for `PhaseState` and `TransitionEvent` to precisely mirror the architectural definitions. Do not use generic string states.\n2. **State Evaluation Loop:** Implement `OrchestrationEngine.ts` utilizing John Ousterhout's \"Deep Modules\" principle. The state transition logic must be fully isolated from any file system I/O. The engine must evaluate transitions solely triggered by explicit, validated message types (`SUBMIT`, `APPROVE`, `REQUEST_CHANGE`, `ESCALATE`) arriving on the bus.\n3. **Message Bus Integration:** Integrate directly with the JSONL parser reading `agency_workspace/inbox.jsonl`.\n4. **File System Watcher Hook:** Hook the FSM evaluations to the `vscode.workspace.createFileSystemWatcher` event specifically watching `inbox.jsonl`. When an append is detected, the event loop must parse the new line and determine if a valid state transition applies based on the current phase and the actor roles.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n- `OrchestrationEngine.ts` is implemented using deep modules, isolating state transition logic from file system I/O.\n- Strict enums for `PhaseState` and `TransitionEvent` are implemented.\n- A comprehensive TDD test suite (100% coverage on transition logic) verifies that the FSM explicitly rejects invalid phase transitions.\n- The `vscode.workspace.createFileSystemWatcher` correctly triggers the FSM evaluation loop upon `inbox.jsonl` appends without crashing or infinite looping.\n\n#### Task 2: Event-Driven Message Bus Implementation\n- **Estimated Hours:** 8 hours\n- **Dependencies:** Task 1\n- **Required Agents:** \n  - `agency-lead-developer`: Orchestrates execution.\n  - `agency-developer`: Implements the JSONL message bus and file watcher logic.\n  - `agency-qa`: Tests concurrency, parsing robustness, and architecture alignment.\n- **Explicit Skills Needed:**\n  - TypeScript File System APIs (`vscode.workspace.createFileSystemWatcher`).\n  - JSONL parsing and string manipulation.\n  - Asynchronous event handling and Node Event Emitter patterns.\n- **Technical Context & SOTA Reference:** SOTA Event-Driven Blackboard Pattern. Native IDE file watchers eliminate the need for heavy external message brokers or polling mechanisms.\n\n**Detailed Functional Description:**\nImplement the Event-Driven Message Bus leveraging the `inbox.jsonl` file as a shared state Blackboard. The architecture must strictly rely on native VS Code APIs for observing state changes to trigger the Orchestration Engine (developed in Task 1).\n\n**Granular Implementation Details:**\n1. **MessageBus wrapper module:** Create `MessageBus.ts` that acts as the core interface for the JSONL file.\n2. **Native Watcher Integration:** Utilize `vscode.workspace.createFileSystemWatcher('**/agency_workspace/inbox.jsonl')` to monitor changes in real-time.\n3. **Parse and Emit:** Upon a change event, read the new line(s), robustly parse the JSON payload into a typed `MessageBusEvent`, and emit the parsed event to the `OrchestrationEngine`.\n4. **Concurrency Safety:** Implement write locking or synchronous append queuing to ensure race conditions do not corrupt the `inbox.jsonl` file when multiple agents attempt to write simultaneously.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n- `MessageBus.ts` successfully watches `inbox.jsonl` and emits typed events without memory leaks.\n- Handles malformed JSON strings gracefully without crashing the extension.\n- 100% TDD test coverage for the JSONL parser and concurrency mechanisms.\n"}]}
{"id":"msg-494","timestamp":"2026-06-21T01:40:00+02:00","from":"agency-lead-developer","to":"agency-architect","phase":"ARCHITECTURE","type":"INFO","ref_doc":"03_architecture.md","message":"I have validated Task 2: Event-Driven Message Bus Implementation against our SOTA research. The use of native VS Code file system watchers correctly eliminates the need for heavy external message brokers and satisfies our lightweight, dependency-free requirement. Please proceed to draft Task 3: Dynamic Context Injector, focusing on IDE-native interception mechanisms. Remember to strictly adhere to the task-by-task cooperational relay protocol.","in_reply_to":"msg-493","status":"RESOLVED","devio_validation_key":"d68ryosqshts1utff5cfd"}
{"id":"msg-495","timestamp":"2026-06-21T01:42:00+02:00","from":"agency-architect","to":"agency-lead-developer","phase":"ARCHITECTURE","type":"SUBMIT","ref_doc":"03_architecture.md","message":"I have drafted Task 3 in the architectural specification (03_architecture.md), focusing strictly on the Dynamic Context Injector leveraging the IDE-native @path resolution mechanism. The task maintains the mandated granularity, specifies required agents and explicit skills, and enforces the strict task-by-task protocol. Please review and validate this task against our SOTA research findings before we proceed.","in_reply_to":"msg-494","status":"RESOLVED","devio_validation_key":"fautq5urprufk3933ub2l6","files":[{"path":"/home/viet/git-perso/MaxApp/Devio/agency_workspace/03_architecture.md","content":"# Architecture Specification: Devio Plugin Complete Revamp\n\n## System Overview\nThe Devio Plugin is being completely revamped to transition from a monolithic, horizontal feature architecture to a structured, vertical, task-oriented workflow based on State-of-the-Art multi-agent systems research. The Orchestration Engine will act as a central router (specifically, the Lead Developer persona acting as the Orchestrator), managing deterministic state transitions through an `inbox.jsonl` message bus. The architecture mandates the use of deep modules, explicit path linking for skill execution, and dynamic context injection using IDE-native `@path` resolution.\n\n## Architecture Diagram\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    Client[Client UI/VS Code] -->|Interacts| CEO[CEO Agent]\n    CEO -->|Proposal/Requirements| LeadDev[Lead Developer / Orchestrator]\n    \n    LeadDev -->|Tasks| Developer[Developer Agent]\n    LeadDev -->|Validates Architecture| Architect[Architect Agent]\n    LeadDev -->|Requests Intel| Researcher[Researcher Agent]\n    Developer -->|Testing/Review| QA[QA Agent]\n    \n    subgraph \"Devio Orchestration Engine (Custom State Machine)\"\n        MessageBus[(inbox.jsonl)]\n        StateManager[State Transitions]\n    end\n    \n    LeadDev <--> MessageBus\n    Developer <--> MessageBus\n    Architect <--> MessageBus\n    QA <--> MessageBus\n    Researcher <--> MessageBus\n    CEO <--> MessageBus\n```\n\n## Component Breakdown\n\n1. **Custom State Machine Orchestrator**\n   - **Purpose:** Governs the agency's lifecycle phases (Brief, Proposal, Architecture, Development, Review, Delivery, Done) and explicitly prevents agents from skipping steps.\n   - **Technology:** TypeScript (Native VS Code Extension).\n   - **Rationale:** A custom FSM is chosen to ensure predictable, deterministic step-by-step executions without introducing massive dependency bloat to the IDE plugin.\n\n2. **Event-Driven Message Bus (`inbox.jsonl`)**\n   - **Purpose:** Serves as the shared \"Blackboard\" where agents read and write state transitions and deliverables.\n   - **Technology:** JSONL file monitored via `vscode.workspace.createFileSystemWatcher`.\n   - **Rationale:** Native VS Code file system watchers provide a lightweight, dependency-free mechanism for real-time state synchronization across the React webview and backend.\n\n3. **Dynamic Context Injector**\n   - **Purpose:** Dynamically references fragmented Markdown skills to be injected into the agent prompt context.\n   - **Technology:** String appending of `@path` syntax in `prompt-builder.ts` leveraging Antigravity IDE native interception.\n   - **Rationale:** By simply appending `Context: @absolute_path`, the Antigravity IDE natively intercepts and embeds file content into the LLM context, bypassing complex file retrieval logic and CDP uploads within the plugin.\n\n## API Contract\n\n```typescript\n// Custom State Machine Interface\ninterface StateTransition {\n    fromPhase: string;\n    toPhase: string;\n    trigger: 'SUBMIT' | 'APPROVE' | 'REQUEST_CHANGE' | 'ESCALATE';\n    actor: string; // The agent executing the transition\n}\n\n// Message Bus Event\ninterface MessageBusEvent {\n    id: string;\n    timestamp: string;\n    from: string;\n    to: string;\n    phase: string;\n    type: string;\n    ref_doc: string | null;\n    message: string;\n    status: 'OPEN' | 'RESOLVED';\n}\n```\n\n## Data Model\n\n| Entity | Attributes | Relationships |\n|:---|:---|:---|\n| Message | id, timestamp, from, to, phase, type, message, ref_doc, status | One-to-Many with State Transitions |\n| Workspace State | currentPhase, activeAgent, pendingTasks | Updated by Orchestrator based on Messages |\n\n## Infrastructure & Deployment\n- **Environment:** Runs purely as a VS Code extension inside the user's IDE.\n- **Deployment:** Packaged as a `.vsix` file using VS Code Extension tooling (`vsce`).\n- **Cloud Costs:** €0.00 (Local execution, relying on client-provided LLM endpoints).\n\n## Phased Implementation Roadmap\n\n> **CRITICAL WORKFLOW RULE:** Tasks are executed strictly one-by-one. We will not proceed to the next task until the current task is completed, validated, and approved. No subsequent tasks will be drafted until Task 1 is validated.\n\n### Phase 1: Custom State Machine\n\n#### Task 1: Core Custom State Machine Bootstrapping\n- **Estimated Hours:** 16 hours\n- **Dependencies:** None\n- **Required Agents:** \n  - `agency-lead-developer`: Orchestrates the implementation, ensures vertical task execution.\n  - `agency-developer`: Implements the FSM and writes the code/tests.\n  - `agency-qa`: Audits the code for security and architecture alignment.\n- **Explicit Skills Needed:**\n  - TypeScript (strict typing, enum creation).\n  - Finite State Machine (FSM) theory and implementation.\n  - Test-Driven Development (TDD).\n  - Deep Module Design (John Ousterhout principles).\n- **Technical Context & SOTA Reference:** SOTA Research - Global Orchestration & Workflow (Explicit FSM over Prompt Chaining) & Professional Coding Standards.\n\n**Detailed Functional Description:**\nImplement the foundational lightweight, custom Finite State Machine (FSM) in the orchestration engine. This code must be written in TypeScript natively within the VS Code extension. The engine must enforce deterministic, vertical task execution by rigorously governing the Devio agency's core lifecycle phases: `Brief`, `Proposal`, `Architecture`, `Development`, `Review`, `Delivery`, and `Done`. The FSM must explicitly reject any attempt by an agent to bypass sequential requirements.\n\n**Granular Implementation Details:**\n1. **Directory Structure & Ubiquitous Language:** Create an isolated domain model representing the state machine. Use strictly defined TypeScript enums for `PhaseState` and `TransitionEvent` to precisely mirror the architectural definitions. Do not use generic string states.\n2. **State Evaluation Loop:** Implement `OrchestrationEngine.ts` utilizing John Ousterhout's \"Deep Modules\" principle. The state transition logic must be fully isolated from any file system I/O. The engine must evaluate transitions solely triggered by explicit, validated message types (`SUBMIT`, `APPROVE`, `REQUEST_CHANGE`, `ESCALATE`) arriving on the bus.\n3. **Message Bus Integration:** Integrate directly with the JSONL parser reading `agency_workspace/inbox.jsonl`.\n4. **File System Watcher Hook:** Hook the FSM evaluations to the `vscode.workspace.createFileSystemWatcher` event specifically watching `inbox.jsonl`. When an append is detected, the event loop must parse the new line and determine if a valid state transition applies based on the current phase and the actor roles.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n- `OrchestrationEngine.ts` is implemented using deep modules, isolating state transition logic from file system I/O.\n- Strict enums for `PhaseState` and `TransitionEvent` are implemented.\n- A comprehensive TDD test suite (100% coverage on transition logic) verifies that the FSM explicitly rejects invalid phase transitions.\n- The `vscode.workspace.createFileSystemWatcher` correctly triggers the FSM evaluation loop upon `inbox.jsonl` appends without crashing or infinite looping.\n\n#### Task 2: Event-Driven Message Bus Implementation\n- **Estimated Hours:** 8 hours\n- **Dependencies:** Task 1\n- **Required Agents:** \n  - `agency-lead-developer`: Orchestrates execution.\n  - `agency-developer`: Implements the JSONL message bus and file watcher logic.\n  - `agency-qa`: Tests concurrency, parsing robustness, and architecture alignment.\n- **Explicit Skills Needed:**\n  - TypeScript File System APIs (`vscode.workspace.createFileSystemWatcher`).\n  - JSONL parsing and string manipulation.\n  - Asynchronous event handling and Node Event Emitter patterns.\n- **Technical Context & SOTA Reference:** SOTA Event-Driven Blackboard Pattern. Native IDE file watchers eliminate the need for heavy external message brokers or polling mechanisms.\n\n**Detailed Functional Description:**\nImplement the Event-Driven Message Bus leveraging the `inbox.jsonl` file as a shared state Blackboard. The architecture must strictly rely on native VS Code APIs for observing state changes to trigger the Orchestration Engine (developed in Task 1).\n\n**Granular Implementation Details:**\n1. **MessageBus wrapper module:** Create `MessageBus.ts` that acts as the core interface for the JSONL file.\n2. **Native Watcher Integration:** Utilize `vscode.workspace.createFileSystemWatcher('**/agency_workspace/inbox.jsonl')` to monitor changes in real-time.\n3. **Parse and Emit:** Upon a change event, read the new line(s), robustly parse the JSON payload into a typed `MessageBusEvent`, and emit the parsed event to the `OrchestrationEngine`.\n4. **Concurrency Safety:** Implement write locking or synchronous append queuing to ensure race conditions do not corrupt the `inbox.jsonl` file when multiple agents attempt to write simultaneously.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n- `MessageBus.ts` successfully watches `inbox.jsonl` and emits typed events without memory leaks.\n- Handles malformed JSON strings gracefully without crashing the extension.\n- 100% TDD test coverage for the JSONL parser and concurrency mechanisms.\n\n#### Task 3: Dynamic Context Injector Implementation\n- **Estimated Hours:** 8 hours\n- **Dependencies:** Task 2\n- **Required Agents:** \n  - `agency-lead-developer`: Orchestrates execution.\n  - `agency-developer`: Implements the context injector logic in prompt building.\n  - `agency-qa`: Tests prompt context formatting and architecture alignment.\n- **Explicit Skills Needed:**\n  - TypeScript string manipulation and template literals.\n  - Knowledge of IDE-native interception (`@path` syntax).\n  - Understanding of LLM context window management.\n- **Technical Context & SOTA Reference:** SOTA Research - Dynamic Context Injection using IDE-native `@path` resolution to optimize LLM context window usage without relying on heavy file processing within the plugin backend.\n\n**Detailed Functional Description:**\nImplement the Dynamic Context Injector within `prompt-builder.ts` to natively leverage the Antigravity IDE's `@absolute_path` resolution capabilities. Rather than the plugin manually loading file contents via Node `fs` and transmitting heavy string payloads, the prompt builder must simply inject the reference (e.g., `Context: @/home/user/path/file.md`) directly into the final string sent to the LLM. The IDE's underlying interception layer will automatically read and embed the target file content into the LLM context.\n\n**Granular Implementation Details:**\n1. **Prompt Builder Refactoring:** Refactor the `prompt-builder.ts` file to generate dynamic prompt strings for agents. When an agent requires context (e.g., from `agency_workspace/00_client_intel.md` or a `SKILL.md` file), construct the prompt line explicitly formatted as `Context: @absolute_path`.\n2. **Absolute Path Resolution:** Implement a robust path resolution utility that dynamically converts workspace-relative paths into fully qualified absolute paths required by the native `@path` interceptor.\n3. **Bypass File Loading Logic:** Ensure that the backend plugin does *not* utilize `fs.readFileSync` or similar Node APIs to read these context files into memory before LLM transmission.\n4. **Agent Skill Binding:** Map the Context Injector to automatically retrieve the absolute paths of the active agent's `SKILL.md` and any associated references defined in their metadata, appending them to the system prompt dynamically based on the current phase.\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n- The `prompt-builder.ts` correctly appends the `@absolute_path` string for required context files (e.g., `00_client_intel.md` and `SKILL.md`).\n- The backend explicitly avoids loading context file contents into memory via Node `fs` methods.\n- The path resolution utility accurately transforms workspace-relative paths into fully qualified absolute paths.\n- A comprehensive TDD test suite verifies that the generated prompt strings contain the exact expected `@path` syntax for all required agent context documents.\n"}]}
