{"id":"msg-511","timestamp":"2026-06-21T02:04:00+02:00","from":"agency-architect","to":"agency-lead-developer","phase":"ARCHITECTURE","type":"SUBMIT","ref_doc":"03_architecture.md","message":"I have drafted Task 3 in 03_architecture.md, detailing the Execution Layer (Recursive Hierarchical Sub-Teams) implementation. I have resolved the open technical decision by defining a strict 3-tier tree structure for Supervisors and Workers, along with their lifecycle boundaries scoped to active sub-tasks. Please validate this task against our SOTA research findings before we proceed.","in_reply_to":"msg-510","status":"RESOLVED","devio_validation_key":"un5l84nj44f6zeywp8suva","files":[{"path":"/home/viet/git-perso/MaxApp/Devio/agency_workspace/03_architecture.md","content":"# Architecture Specification: Devio Hybrid Multi-Agent System\n\n## System Overview\nThe Devio agency architecture is undergoing a complete recoding based on State-of-the-Art (SOTA) multi-agent system principles. It transitions from rigid request-response chains and a flat agent roster to a Hybrid Architecture (Event-Based Hierarchical Blackboard). The system operates on three foundational layers:\n1. **Context Layer (Shared Blackboard)**: A centralized knowledge repository that stores the current state, constraints, and workspace context. All agents read from and write to this single source of truth to collaborate asynchronously.\n2. **Communication Layer (Event-Driven Orchestration)**: An asynchronous event bus where state updates to the Blackboard trigger immutable events, using implicit invocation to decouple agents.\n3. **Execution Layer (Recursive Hierarchical Sub-Teams)**: A tree-structured hierarchy where a top-level orchestrator delegates goals to specialized Supervisor agents, who in turn manage their own execution loops with specialized Worker agents.\n\n## Architecture Diagram\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    subgraph Communication Layer\n        EB[Event Bus]\n    end\n    \n    subgraph Context Layer\n        BB[(Centralized Blackboard)]\n    end\n    \n    subgraph Execution Layer\n        TO[Top-Level Orchestrator]\n        TS[Technical Supervisor]\n        DS[Design Supervisor]\n        CW[Code Worker]\n        QAW[QA Worker]\n        \n        TO -->|Delegates| TS\n        TO -->|Delegates| DS\n        TS -->|Manages| CW\n        TS -->|Manages| QAW\n    end\n    \n    TO <-->|Reads/Writes| BB\n    TS <-->|Reads/Writes| BB\n    CW <-->|Reads/Writes| BB\n    \n    BB -->|Triggers State Changes| EB\n    EB -->|Notifies| TO\n    EB -->|Notifies| TS\n```\n\n## Component Breakdown\n- **Centralized Blackboard**: Purpose is to maintain system state, resolve context degradation (the \"phone game\" problem), and ensure a unified memory space. Uses an in-memory or lightweight persistent DB store. \n- **Event Bus**: Purpose is to enable asynchronous, decoupled communication. Technology choice: Node.js native `EventEmitter` or a persistent local event log.\n- **Hierarchical Agents**: Autonomous agent nodes organized as Supervisors and Workers, replacing the monolithic flat roster.\n\n## API Contract & Data Model\n*(Deferred to subsequent tasks pending Blackboard implementation specifics)*\n\n## Infrastructure & Deployment\n- Node.js (Current LTS version) environment.\n- Native filesystem or a lightweight local DB (e.g., SQLite 3) for Blackboard storage to ensure fast read/writes without heavy cloud dependencies.\n\n## Implementation Task List\n\n> **CRITICAL WORKFLOW RULE:** Tasks are executed strictly one-by-one. No subsequent tasks will be drafted until this current task is completed, validated, and approved.\n\n### Task 1: Core Blackboard Context Layer Implementation\n- **Estimated Hours:** 4 hours\n- **Dependencies:** None\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - A structured `Blackboard` class is implemented, capable of storing global state, workspace constraints, and agent consensus.\n  - The Blackboard exposes explicit `read(query)` and `write(key, data)` interfaces.\n  - 100% test coverage for the Blackboard state management logic.\n- **Explicit Research Cross-Referencing:** This directly addresses the `00_client_intel.md` research finding: \"Replace linear message passing with a persistent, embedding-based knowledge layer (Blackboard) that stores the current state, constraints, and workspace context.\"\n\n### Task 2: Communication Layer (Event-Driven Orchestration) & Event Bus Implementation\n- **Estimated Hours:** 8 hours\n- **Dependencies:** Task 1 (Core Blackboard)\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - An `EventBus` class wrapping Node.js native `EventEmitter` is implemented to handle asynchronous triggers.\n  - The `EventBus` exposes strict typed `subscribe(event, handler)` and `publish(event, payload)` interfaces.\n  - The Blackboard (from Task 1) is wired to automatically publish immutable events upon state modifications (e.g., `BLACKBOARD_UPDATED`).\n  - 100% TDD test coverage for the Event Bus pub/sub logic and its reliable integration with the Blackboard.\n- **Explicit Research Cross-Referencing:** This addresses the `00_client_intel.md` requirement: \"When an agent updates the Blackboard, it triggers immutable events on an event bus. The system uses 'implicit invocation' rather than explicit routing.\"\n\n### Task 3: Execution Layer (Recursive Hierarchical Sub-Teams) Implementation\n- **Estimated Hours:** 12 hours\n- **Dependencies:** Task 2 (Communication Layer)\n- **Acceptance Criteria:**\n  - A `BaseAgent` class is implemented that connects directly to the Event Bus and Blackboard.\n  - A Top-Level Orchestrator agent is defined to decompose high-level goals into sub-tasks.\n  - Supervisor agents (Technical Supervisor, Design Supervisor) are implemented to manage specific execution loops and coordinate Worker agents.\n  - Worker agents (Code Worker, QA Worker) are implemented to handle specialized, granular execution tasks.\n  - Supervisors must correctly manage the lifecycle of their assigned Workers, aggregating their results before writing the final consensus back to the Blackboard.\n  - 100% test coverage for the lifecycle management and delegation logic.\n- **Explicit Research Cross-Referencing:** This addresses the `00_client_intel.md` requirement: \"Replace the flat list of agents with a recursive supervisor-worker hierarchy. A top-level orchestrator decomposes goals into sub-tasks and delegates them to specialized supervisor agents... The supervisors manage their local execution loops and write the final consensus back to the Blackboard.\"\n\n## Open Technical Decisions\n- **Event Bus Technology**: **Resolved.** We will use the Node.js native `EventEmitter` (memory-based) for the Event Bus. Because the Blackboard serves as the persistent single source of truth (Context Layer), the events themselves can remain transient. This ensures the required decoupled, implicit invocation while remaining extremely lightweight, avoiding the I/O overhead of a persistent DB queue for high-frequency transient events.\n- **Agent Hierarchy Definitions**: **Resolved.** The system will use a strict 3-tier tree structure: Tier 1 (Top-Level Orchestrator) -> Tier 2 (Domain Supervisors: Technical, Design) -> Tier 3 (Specialized Workers: Code, QA). Lifecycle boundaries are strictly scoped: a worker's lifecycle is bound to its parent supervisor's active sub-task. Supervisors are responsible for spawning, monitoring, and terminating their workers based on Event Bus triggers, aggregating their output, and writing the final state back to the Blackboard.\n"}]}
